Name: Tyler McFarland
Date: 10/1/2026
Environment: opsys (Linux), vi

How to compile the project:
   Type 'make'

Example of how to run the project:
   ./oss -n 5 -s 3 -t 4.5 -i 0.2

How it works:
oss makes a simulated clock in shared memory and launches workers with fork
and exec. It never sleeps. Every loop it moves the clock forward, prints the
process table every half second of simulated time, checks if a worker
finished with waitpid (WNOHANG), and launches a new worker if -n, -s and -i
allow it. When a worker finishes its spot in the table gets cleared and the
next worker uses it. At the end it prints how many workers ran and how long
they ran combined.

Each worker reads the clock, adds the time it was given to get when it should
stop, and keeps checking the clock until that time passes. It prints every
time the seconds change and once more when it terminates.

oss stops after 60 real seconds or when you hit Ctrl-C. Either way it kills
the workers and frees the shared memory. If shared memory ever gets left
behind run ./memclean.sh
